<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Borromeo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1538">1538</a>–<a href="/years/1584">1584</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Carlo Borromeo, Saint Charles Borromeo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Borromeo was archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> from 1564 and a leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>. A nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-iv">Pope Pius IV</a>, he helped bring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> to its close and then enforced its decrees in his large diocese through synods, visitations, seminaries and strict discipline of the clergy. He cared for the sick during the plague of 1576–1577 and was canonised in 1610.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-borromeo.jpg" alt="Charles Borromeo" width="320" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Ambrogio Figino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carlo_Borromeo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles Borromeo was born in the castle of Arona on Lake Maggiore on 2 October 1538. His father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a>, was Count of Arona, and his mother, Margaret, belonged to the Milanese branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-medici">House of Medici</a>. The Borromeo family was one of the oldest and wealthiest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>. Charles received the tonsure at about twelve and, when given the income of a rich Benedictine abbey, told his father that whatever exceeded the cost of his preparation for the church belonged to the poor. He studied law at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-pavia">University of Pavia</a> and took his doctorate on 6 December 1559.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When his uncle was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-iv">Pope Pius IV</a> on 25 December 1559, he summoned Borromeo to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and made him a cardinal on 31 January 1560. He organised the third and last session of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> in 1562 and 1563 and had a large share in the Tridentine Catechism. The death of his brother Federico in November 1562, and his contacts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> and Theatines, led him to a stricter life and to put into practice a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s duties as the Council set them out. He was formally appointed archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1564 and entered the city in September 1565.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> was the largest archdiocese in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, with over 3,000 clergy and 800,000 people, and no archbishop had been present for about 80 years. Borromeo found indulgences and offices sold and monasteries in disorder. He made pastoral visits and, believing that abuses arose from ignorant clergy, founded seminaries and colleges, the Oblates of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a> and the first Sunday school classes. Some members of the Humiliati, an order that opposed him, plotted against his life, and a shot fired at him in his chapel missed.</p><p>In 1576 famine struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, followed by plague, and the governor and many nobles fled. Borromeo stayed, tried to feed 60,000 to 70,000 people daily and went into debt to do so. In 1583, as apostolic visitor to the Swiss cantons, he repressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> in the valleys. In the Valle Mesolcina 150 people were arrested for witchcraft, and the civil authorities condemned eleven women and the provost of Roveredo to be burned alive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Borromeo's uncompromising stance brought him into conflict with secular leaders, priests and even the pope. He fell ill in 1584 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on 3 November, aged 46. Paul V canonised him on 1 November 1610, and he is the patron saint of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, catechists and seminarians.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles Borromeo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Spanish rule the economy suffered from heavy taxation and the plague of 1630, while the Counter-Reformation archbishop <b>Charles Borromeo</b> reshaped parish life.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Carlo Borromeo</b>, Archbishop of Milan (1538–1584), set an example to bishops by visiting the remotest parishes and instilling high standards, and pressed for the council’s decrees, in music too, to be put quickly into practice.</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · People</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Arona, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.76,8.56&amp;year=1567" aria-label="Arona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Charles Borromeo"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5509 6173h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5509 6173h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.76,8.56&amp;year=1567">What was here in 1567? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
